From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <DCHEM88@hotmail.it>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:40:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nb/intel/haswell/nri: Test the memory after training, and
 train again once

The MRC ends a training with a memory test (MEMTST) and a margin check
that redo the training when it is not good enough; the native RAM init
saves whatever the training steps produced and boots on it, and the
first sign of a marginal training is the OS.

Add a memory test task at the end of the training schedule, behind
HASWELL_NRI_MEMORY_TEST: with the REUT, every bank of every rank is
written with the victim/aggressor patterns of the WDB and read back,
the first miscompare stops the test, and the lanes in error are logged.
The addressing is the one of the ECC scrub, the sequence and the data
the ones of the alias check: a write subsequence and a read one over
the same cachelines, the WDB patterns driven by the LFSRs, which
setup_wdb() seeds alike for writes and reads. It runs with the training
only: a fast boot or an S3 resume restores the values it checked and
does not touch the memory contents.

When a training step or the test fails, train once more from scratch
before giving up, which raminit_main had as a TODO.

TEST=On a ThinkPad W541 (i7-4810MQ, four 8 GiB DDR3L-1600 dual-rank
SO-DIMMs): after a cold boot training the test passed on the four ranks
of both channels, first with the WDB patterns from the bit buffer, then
with the LFSRs as here; the next boots took the fast path without it.

Co-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018n3yuWwDLW63F4vdf7ZFTv
